{"authored" [{"question" "q-38703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "53fd71314705cc27c121d2e590ddcf2bf95e3f37fe26ee975b9f6452012f9fd1" "basis" 1867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-38703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "53fd71314705cc27c121d2e590ddcf2bf95e3f37fe26ee975b9f6452012f9fd1" "basis" 1867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-38703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.dif