le_sha256" "53fd71314705cc27c121d2e590ddcf2bf95e3f37fe26ee975b9f6452012f9fd1" "basis" 1867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+1) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+2A-4I-5{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "53fd71314705cc27c121d2e590ddcf2bf95e3f37fe26ee975b9f6452012f9fd1" "basis" 1867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107" "basis" 1867 "verification" "/home/jake