_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "52afb5275bd0959a2d515d3f40a045f29dca7f07c8dc62c8195a0317cd10c970"} {"question" "q-85929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "49I-12A+{A}^{2}"] ["math" "9I+2A+{A}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{58}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{58}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-40168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-40168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+A-7I+2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 2{A}^{-1} & =7I-A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-40168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "52afb5275bd0959a2d515d3f40a045f29dca7f07c8dc62c8195a0317cd10c970"} {"question" "q-40168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-40168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2I-A-2{A}^{2}"] ["math" "9I-A-{A}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{3}{2}{A}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{7}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-40168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-140531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-140531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-2) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}-2{A}^{2}-11A+24I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}-2{A}^{2}-11A+24I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-2A-11I+24{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 24{A}^{-1} & =11I+2A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-140531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "52afb5275bd0959a2d515d3f40a045f29dca7f07c8dc62c8195a0317cd10c970"} {"question" "q-140531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-140531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "24I+\\frac{1}{12}A+\\frac{1}{6}{A}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{12}I+\\frac{1}{3}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A+\\frac{1}{12}{A}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{24}I-\\frac{1}{6}A+\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-140531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-7A+8I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-7A+8I) & =O \\\\ A-7I+8{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{8}I-\\frac{1}{8}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{7}{8},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{8}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{7}{8}+(-\\frac{1}{8})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "52afb5275bd0959a2d515d3f40a045f29dca7f07c8dc62c8195a0317cd10c970"} {"question" "q-85928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+1) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+2A-4I-5{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "52afb5275bd0959a2d515d3f40a045f29dca7f07c8dc62c8195a0317cd10c970"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2I+4A+{A}^{2}"] ["math" "-5I-4A-5{A}^{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{4}{5}{A}^{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{1}{5}A+\\frac{2}{5}{A}^{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{21}-16{x}^{19}+6x-3$, find $f(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{19}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-16\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{21}\\,-\\,16{x}^{19}\\,+\\,6x\\,\\,-\\,\\,3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{21}$ | $-$ | $16{x}^{19}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $6x$ | $-$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(6x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{19}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =6A-3I \\\\  & =6[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "52afb5275bd0959a2d515d3f40a045f29dca7f07c8dc62c8195a0317cd10c970"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 0 \\\\ 72 & -27\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 27 \\\\ 0 & -72\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}27 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -21\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-140606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ then ${A}^{4}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-140606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $3x$ | $+$ | $11$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+3x-2\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | $+$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $2{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $+$ | $2{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $9{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $11{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $11{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $33x$ | $-$ | $22$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $-$ | $39x$ | $+$ | $22$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{4}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =q(x)p(x)+(-39x+22),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{2}-3x+11.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=q(A)p(A)+(-39A+22I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & =q(A)p(A)+(-39A+22I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-39A+22I) \\\\  & =-39A+22I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-140606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "52afb5275bd0959a2d515d3f40a045f29dca7f07c8dc62c8195a0317cd10c970"} {"question" "q-140606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-140606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10A+7I"] ["math" "-12A+53I"] ["math" "-21A+2I"] ["math" "-39A+22I"] ["math" "30A+15I"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-140606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-39A+22I"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-x-20,$ then ${A}^{3}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-x-20,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,x$ | $+$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-x-20\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{3}\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $20x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $20x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $x$ | $-$ | $20$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $21x$ | $+$ | $20$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{3}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =q(x)p(x)+(21x+20),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)=x+1.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{3}=q(A)p(A)+(21A+20I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =q(A)p(A)+(21A+20I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(21A+20I) \\\\  & =21A+20I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "52afb5275bd0959a2d515d3f40a045f29dca7f07c8dc62c8195a0317cd10c970"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-20A-21I"] ["math" "-21A+20I"] ["math" "20A+21I"] ["math" "21A+20I"] ["math" "21A-20I"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21A+20I"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{22}-{x}^{20}+3x-2\\,$, find $f(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+8 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{20}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-1\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{22}\\,-\\,{x}^{20}\\,+\\,3x\\,-\\,2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{22}$ | $-$ | ${x}^{20}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $3x$ | $-$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(3x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{20}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =3A-2I \\\\  & =3[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "52afb5275bd0959a2d515d3f40a045f29dca7f07c8dc62c8195a0317cd10c970"} {"question" "q-37835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+x-6,$ then ${A}^{3}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+x-6,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,x$ | $-$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+x-6\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{3}\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | $+$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $x$ | $+$ | $6$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $7x$ | $-$ | $6$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{3}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-6),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)=x-1.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{3}=q(A)p(A)+(7A-6I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-6I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-6I) \\\\  & =7A-6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "52afb5275bd0959a2d515d3f40a045f29dca7f07c8dc62c8195a0317cd10c970"} {"question" "q-37773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-7A+6I"] ["math" "6A-7I"] ["math" "7A-6I"] ["math" "A-16I"] ["math" "A-I"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "q-37773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7A-6I"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "51da58a67643ee915b8cacd53c1ca2a82be583c60f64763f09ea4d3eb6812ebb"} {"question" "e-5894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/assets/cb48b48ef82e5d6cfe903488e80a8cbd48e286c864ba387450e01430e718d77c.png) find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/example-5894.json" "file_sha256" "c232bedef55fe7ecb13cf38468ba99bc0c71361e36a863e522608dccbb9256c4"} {"question" "e-5894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda),$ i.e., the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/example-5894.json" "file_sha256" "c232bedef55fe7ecb13cf38468ba99bc0c71361e36a863e522608dccbb9256c4"} {"question" "e-5511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\textrm{tr}(A)=-4$ and $\\textrm{det}(A)=-18.$ If $A^2 = xA + yI$ for constants $x$ and $y,$ then find the value of $x+y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/example-5511.json" "file_sha256" "bcb30d0842bf6dad542b6e92bfab23d81a797bcb62bd66e8bb61a4d77471566d"} {"question" "e-5511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2-\\textrm{tr}(A)\\lambda+\\textrm{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-4A+18I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y = -4+18 = 14.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/example-5511.json" "file_sha256" "bcb30d0842bf6dad542b6e92bfab23d81a797bcb62bd66e8bb61a4d77471566d"} {"question" "e-5510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/assets/93c4cdd7a16be0f08bd161b97dde5002a3f12dee2febd3857fe9da289f9a9c4a.png) and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/example-5510.json" "file_sha256" "ebf5bf5b9ed90c40994260f4b29528e4944c61ccd898094154a11158efcac4b0"} {"question" "e-5510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}+A-2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}+A-2I) & =O \\\\ A+I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $q=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/example-5510.json" "file_sha256" "ebf5bf5b9ed90c40994260f4b29528e4944c61ccd898094154a11158efcac4b0"} {"question" "e-5512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/assets/7102b8bb9499f228ac58b16b7fd977fbc2d95e1ef87c21742f14255d4cdbc992.png) and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1,$ find $f(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/example-5512.json" "file_sha256" "b8ffb2164d625db7d423246d2a0d877de5eec517b80e7c399e36e0cf39c01912"} {"question" "e-5512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+15 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\:\\:x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x^2-1\\:$ | $\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\, - \\, x^{10}  +  2x  -  1 {\\phantom{|}} }$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $x^{12}$ | $-$ | $x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $2x$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{10}({x}^{2}-1)+(2x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x) = x^{10}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-I) \\\\  & =2A-I \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/example-5512.json" "file_sha256" "b8ffb2164d625db7d423246d2a0d87